<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Diocese of Hispania</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 300–5th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> · Also known as Diocese of the Spains</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Diocese of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a>, or Diocese of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spains</a>, was a civil administrative unit of the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> created by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a>'s reforms around 300. It grouped the provinces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a>, among them Lusitania, Baetica, Carthaginiensis, Tarraconensis and Gallaecia, with Mauretania Tingitana in north <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balearic-islands">Balearic Islands</a>. A vicar, probably resident at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emerita-augusta">Emerita Augusta</a>, governed it under the praetorian prefecture of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/diocese-of-hispania.jpg" alt="Diocese of Hispania" width="320" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tomisti · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dioecesis_Hispaniae_locator_map.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
